{
	"edges":[
		{"id":"eb25af6d8dadb1ee","fromNode":"47a9580bd60c389c","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"bfa1a54f200ffb8b","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"657a38998453d613","fromNode":"bfa1a54f200ffb8b","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"f5b0c530ea422a37","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"56ac8c3481638845","fromNode":"8150405565c7f515","fromSide":"left","toNode":"f5b0c530ea422a37","toSide":"right"},
		{"id":"b92e8e34ef2fdd95","fromNode":"47a9580bd60c389c","fromSide":"top","toNode":"faabc82dcdbcc04c","toSide":"bottom"},
		{"id":"ba7e3a0671bdbd60","fromNode":"c53ef10a1d864389","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"2c50961a77b338bb","toSide":"top"},
		{"id":"96848fc0cc0bbc1b","fromNode":"2c50961a77b338bb","fromSide":"bottom","toNode":"518be1ffc0abfb4f","toSide":"top"}
	],
	"nodes":[
		{
			"id":"47a9580bd60c389c",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"## Free Will Exists",
			"type":"text",
			"x":-80,
			"y":240,
			"width":220,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"bfa1a54f200ffb8b",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"Everything must be deterministic, or random. Law of excluded middle. Neither of those can lead to free will",
			"type":"text",
			"x":-53,
			"y":400,
			"width":293,
			"height":121
		},
		{
			"id":"faabc82dcdbcc04c",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"There are immaterial things such as math and logic, as well as our consciousness",
			"type":"text",
			"x":-80,
			"y":20,
			"width":328,
			"height":96,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"c53ef10a1d864389",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"If there is a causal connection between the mind and the brain (which there has to be), it's not clear in which direction that connection goes",
			"type":"text",
			"x":-512,
			"y":745,
			"width":392,
			"height":115
		},
		{
			"id":"2c50961a77b338bb",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"If there is an author who writes a book, can a book write an author? In other words, it seems more likely that a mind could cause physical changes in the brain than physical changes in the brain can cause thoughts",
			"type":"text",
			"x":-249,
			"y":960,
			"width":389,
			"height":131
		},
		{
			"id":"ea75d024e7f210b9",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"Also, it seems that a large part of our decision making is not necessarily ours - when we get into a flow state we are somewhat unconscious, our dream state is really weird and do we really make decisions there besides lucid dreaming, and our cognitive functions are very much impacted by the amount of sleep and nutrition and exercise etc. At the very least, the decision making is strange and complex and not uniformly in charge",
			"type":"text",
			"x":-575,
			"y":80,
			"width":455,
			"height":240,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"784f68663ecbd577",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"René Descartes - Cogito ergo sum. That only works if our thoughts are something?",
			"type":"text",
			"x":248,
			"y":195,
			"width":312,
			"height":105
		},
		{
			"id":"8150405565c7f515",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"That is another basic assumption that will have to be included in the general worldview simplicity comparison, but that makes sense",
			"type":"text",
			"x":419,
			"y":521,
			"width":283,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"f5b0c530ea422a37",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"This is a false dichotomy. There might be a third category, that is essentially defined as free will or choice. I'm not sure where God would fall into this, but God created us to be partly deterministic (our physical bodies), and to have free will (with our soul)",
			"type":"text",
			"x":-47,
			"y":623,
			"width":374,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"ea9a9762e627af5e",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"The Hard Problem of Consciousness",
			"type":"text",
			"x":-475,
			"y":1201,
			"width":315,
			"height":79
		},
		{
			"id":"a07a0b1b192edad7",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"# NDEs",
			"type":"text",
			"x":-404,
			"y":1359,
			"width":155,
			"height":61
		},
		{
			"id":"518be1ffc0abfb4f",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"text":"In that scenario, of course the direction is clear. However than analogy just doesn't map onto the idea of mind vs brain. And if a mind is immaterial, why can't my mind control your hand? It's not spatially located, although perhaps constrained in some other way.",
			"type":"text",
			"x":-98,
			"y":1170,
			"width":517,
			"height":141
		}
	]
}